This review corrects the claim in [the original incident](/kb/incidents/4471) that one fix covers every report. It uses the 4 October 2026 export; it does not establish that the live queue is unchanged.

## Engineering update: closed with different outcomes

The [course's engineering update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows) now marks #4471 closed on 05 Oct. The parser is **fixed**, with `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. The booking-v3 hint is **won't fix** because the booking-v3 form was retired. Closure does not mean both bugs were fixed: the second path was retired. The report counts below remain the historical export's coverage.

Our [parser ticket](https://github.com/chrapson551/mega-s01e03/issues/1) is completed. Our [hint ticket](https://github.com/chrapson551/mega-s01e03/issues/2) is closed as not planned, with the retirement reason retained.

## Coverage

<ReviewedCauses />

## Two distinct problems

The shared parser reads month first even for tenants who write day first. R-41200 describes 7 April becoming 4 July. Resolve the tenant from the directory; the reporter's browser locale and intake tag are guesses, not authoritative settings.

In the export, the booking-v3 form prints MM/DD/YYYY, but reads the input according to the tenant locale. A day-first tenant following that hint supplies a month-first date that the form misreads. Copper Studio, Alder Physio, and Pale Harbour ran this version. Changing only the shared parser would not correct the hint; engineering instead retired booking-v3.

The five flagged reports describe unusual symptoms: one-day shifts, a date that differs between Lisbon and Toronto, and an invite reportedly arriving before booking. They remain part of the parser ticket under the supplied tenant-and-channel classification. Record them as diagnostic notes and obtain original input, invite payloads, and dispatch logs. These symptoms alone do not establish a third problem or prove a timezone, daylight-saving, or dispatch defect.

## The queue kept moving

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Received dates and merge dates measure different events. The bulk merge in July does not mean the failures began in July. New reports continue through the export cutoff. A queued fix and a known status do not show resolution.

## Triage from now on

Read the body, identify the product and tenant, check its locale and form version, and compare the actual failure mechanism. Merge only when that evidence matches. A similar subject can hide different bugs; different subjects can describe the same bug. Keep uncertain reports visible until investigated.

The accepted [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is preserved as history. [Proposed decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-evidence) replaces subject-only merging if adopted. The old triage page reflects that earlier policy, not a validated rule for this review. Review dates and stale warnings indicate when a page needs checking; they are not proof that its claims are correct.

The rendered triage explorer contradicts its surrounding rule: two Alder Physio reports with the same subject have different causes when one arrives through the form and the other through the app. Its examples also show different subjects with the same cause, and identical subjects from an unknown tenant that must not be merged without investigation. The triage page was last reviewed in June 2024 and now shows a stale warning.

## Sources and limits

Sources: reports-4471.json, all tenant files, desk-notes.json, causes.ts, locales.ts, CauseTable.svelte, QueueTimeline.svelte, and decision 0001. Engineering outcomes come from the course page and are recorded in desk-update.json. The numbers above are computed from those files at render time. The booking application and dispatch logs are absent, so reproduction steps in the issue bodies require engineering verification. The public repository contains the issue coverage lists and this page's source and rendered HTML.

